# M7 — Training Decision Engine (mid-week adaptation + durability)

> Drop this file in the repo root next to PLAN.md. In Claude Code:
> "Read CLAUDE.md, PLAN.md, then implement M7-DECISION-ENGINE.md phase by phase."
>
> Origin: gap analysis of Ben's real coaching-conversation workflow vs. the shipped
> M0–M6 system. Core finding: the app regenerates plans weekly, but Ben's actual
> interaction loop is per-run — "I did this, how should the plan change now?"
> Everything below preserves PLAN.md §1: **code computes, the LLM judges.**

## What is explicitly NOT in scope

- No manual run-log screen (HealthKit already captures distance/pace — a manual
  3-field log would be a regression).
- No generic daily check-in buttons (removed in M6 as cheap; do not resurrect).
- No LLM-estimated fitness/durability/probability numbers — all new metrics are
  engine math; the LLM only narrates them.

## Phase 1 — Per-run feel (the one field HealthKit can't provide)

The third field of Ben's natural log ("how did it feel") as structured data
attached to the run, distinct from free-text notes.

- Migration: `runs.feel` int nullable, 1–5 (1 = terrible … 5 = great).
  Keep `runs.notes` as-is (subjective detail stays LLM territory).
- iOS: in RunDetailView, a 5-segment feel picker above the notes editor.
  When a fresh run syncs (run date == today) and `feel` is null, the run card
  on the home screen shows the picker inline — one tap, no sheet.
- Server: `feel` rides along with notes in all advise/chat/plan prompts as a
  pre-formatted string ("felt: 4/5"), never a bare number (gotcha #4).
- Engine: expose `last_run_feel` in state; no scoring logic yet (Phase 2 uses it).

Exit test: tap feel on a run → value persists in PB → appears in the next
chat prompt (verify with `LLM_PROVIDER=mock` + prompt logging).

## Phase 2 — Mid-week re-plan (the core of M7)

Trigger: a run syncs and reconciles against a planned workout, OR a planned
workout flips to `skipped`. Decide deterministically whether the *remainder of
this week* needs adjustment; only then spend an LLM call.

- `plan.replanRemainder(app, state, trigger)` in plan.js:
  - **Code decides IF**: replan only when one of these fires —
    (a) planned workout skipped and ≥2 non-rest days remain this week;
    (b) completed run deviates >25% in distance from plan;
    (c) `feel ≤ 2` on a quality day (T/I/R/MP);
    (d) `feel = 5` on a quality day AND light is 🟢 AND week is under cap.
    Otherwise: log "no replan needed" and stop. Quiet is fine; ghosting isn't —
    reuse the M5 logging pattern.
  - **Code decides the rails**: remaining-week km cap = weeklyCapKm minus km
    already done; injured → everything rest; 🔴 → no quality remaining; only
    future days of the current week may change; race day immovable.
  - **LLM decides**: which remaining days change and how to phrase it. Same
    sanitizer/repair path as plan-week (repairs logged into rationale).
  - Changed workouts get `status: modified`, original preserved in description
    (same convention as pullTodayIfRed).
- Wire the trigger into the existing reconcile path in main.pb.js (morning cron
  + on-sync), not a new cron. One replan max per day (cost-conscious).
- Coach message of kind `plan_change` explaining what moved and why — the
  "Let's increase Saturday from 14 to 16km" interaction, but rail-bounded.
- iOS: modified workouts already render; ensure the plan_change message surfaces
  in chat on next open.

Exit test (offline, mock LLM): fixture week + simulated easy-feeling threshold
run under 🟢 → Saturday LR grows within cap; simulated skipped workout →
remaining days reshuffle; 🔴 fixture → replan produces no quality days.
Add to `test-engine-local.sh`.

## Phase 3 — Distance-based workout rail

Ben's stated preference: never count minutes or reps of time. Make it a rail,
not a prompt suggestion.

- Planner + replan prompts: interval/repetition workouts MUST be prescribed as
  distance reps (e.g. "6 × 600m", "3 × 2km"), never time-based.
- Sanitizer: regex-detect time-based prescriptions ("min", "minute", "'", "sec")
  in I/R/T descriptions; convert using zones_sec (rep duration × zone pace →
  round to nearest 100m) and log the repair into the rationale.

Exit test: deliberately time-based mock LLM fixture → sanitizer emits
distance-based description; fixture added to test-engine-local.sh.

## Phase 4 — Durability score (engine math; the 30km-fade limiter)

`runs.splits` exists in the schema but engine.js never reads it. Ben's known
limiter is late-race fade/cramping — make it measurable.

- engine.js additions, computed over long runs (≥15km) in the last 90d:
  - **pace decay**: 2nd-half avg pace vs 1st-half, from splits (skip runs
    without splits — the M1 audit found Runkeeper writes distance+HR; verify
    splits coverage empirically first and degrade gracefully to halves-by-
    cumulative-distance if per-km splits are absent).
  - **HR drift**: 2nd-half avg HR vs 1st-half at comparable pace (aerobic
    decoupling proxy). Requires per-split HR; if unavailable, omit — never
    estimate.
  - **durability 0–100**: 100 minus weighted decay+drift penalties; flag 🟡
    when decay >5% on multiple recent long runs.
- Surface in `for_llm` (pre-formatted strings) and as a Trends chart
  (per-long-run decay %, with the score in the chart commentary facts).
- Heat: if HKWorkoutMetadata temperature is present on the workout, sync it to
  a new `runs.temp_c` field and have the engine annotate decay readings taken
  above 25°C ("expected in heat — judge by effort"). If Runkeeper doesn't
  write it (likely), the field stays null and nothing breaks — check the field
  audit output before building UI around it.

Exit test: hand-computed fixture (synthetic splits with known 6% decay) →
engine returns expected decay + flag; 14/14 → N/N in test-engine-local.sh.

## Phase 5 — Goal trajectory (deterministic "can I break 3:10?")

- engine.js: from goal race time + distance → required VDOT (Daniels–Gilbert
  inverse, already have the forward math in Models.swift — port to engine).
- Trajectory: linear fit of effort-VDOT over the last 120d of quality efforts
  → projected VDOT at race date → status `on_track | borderline | off_track`
  (borderline = within ±1 VDOT point).
- Surface: Plan tab header ("needs 51.2 — currently 47.7, trending +0.4/mo →
  borderline") + `for_llm` strings so the coach narrates honestly. No
  percentage probabilities — a trend + gap is honest; "73% chance of sub-3:10"
  would be false precision.

Exit test: fixtures for clearly-on-track and clearly-off-track histories.

## Phase 6 — Return-to-run rail (current reality: Ben is injured)

More urgent than anything above on the calendar, smallest in code.

- profile gains `return_to_run_date` (nullable). While injured OR within 4
  weeks of that date, weeklyCapKm follows a graduated ramp: wk1 = 30% of
  pre-injury chronic, +20%/wk after, all-easy (no quality) for the first 2
  weeks regardless of what the LLM proposes — sanitizer enforces.
- Persona note: post-injury tone — 🟢 light does not mean "push" during the
  ramp window.

Exit test: injured-recently fixture → generated week is short + quality-free
even with a rail-breaking mock response.

## Ordering & deploy

Phases are independent except 2 depends on 1 (feel as trigger input). Suggested
order given the injury: **6 → 1 → 2 → 4 → 3 → 5.** Each phase: migration (if
any) → hooks → fixtures in test-engine-local.sh → iOS → Ben runs
`./scripts/deploy-server.sh ben@192.168.1.236` + Xcode install. All offline-
testable via LLM_PROVIDER=mock per house rules; destructive infra stays Ben's.